I think you’re asking about all the data types supported in YAML. Here’s a complete list with examples.

---

1. Scalars (single values)

Strings

```yaml
name: Ali
greeting: "Hello, world!"
description: |
  This is a multi-line
  string block.
```

Numbers

```yaml
age: 25
price: 19.99
scientific: 1.2e3   # 1200
```

Booleans

```yaml
active: true
disabled: false
```

(Also accepts yes/no, on/off)

Null

```yaml
middle_name: null
spouse: ~          # also means null
```

Dates & Times (usually parsed as strings unless a schema is used)

```yaml
birthday: 1998-07-23
meeting_time: 2026-07-13T09:30:00+08:00
```

---

2. Sequences (lists / arrays)

```yaml
hobbies:
  - reading
  - swimming
  - hiking
```

Can also be written inline:

```yaml
numbers: [1, 2, 3]
```

---

3. Mappings (dictionaries / objects)

```yaml
address:
  city: Kuala Lumpur
  postcode: 50000
```

Inline mapping:

```yaml
point: {x: 10, y: 20}
```

---

4. Special types

Anchors & Aliases (reuse data)

```yaml
defaults: &default
  timeout: 30
  retry: 3

service_a:
  <<: *default
  name: auth

service_b:
  <<: *default
  name: payment
```

Tags (explicit data type hints)

```yaml
explicit_string: !!str 123
explicit_int: !!int "456"
```

Binary (Base64 encoded)

```yaml
file_data: !!binary |
  R0lGODlhDAAMAIQAAP//9/X17unp5W...
```

Sets (unique values, using ? syntax)

```yaml
unique_colors:
  ? red
  ? green
  ? blue
```

---

5. Multi-document YAML (using --- and ...)

```yaml
---
document: 1
...
---
document: 2
...
```

---

