Got it — after “All data”, you're now asking about “All URLs” in YAML.

There is no special URL data type in YAML. A URL is just a string. However, there are some best practices and gotchas when writing URLs in YAML.

---

Basic URL (no quotes needed if it’s simple)

```yaml
website: https://example.com
```

✅ Works fine because it doesn't contain special YAML characters that break parsing.

---

When you must use quotes

If a URL contains characters that YAML could misinterpret — like :, #, {, }, [, ], ,, &, *, ?, |, >, !, % — you should quote it.

```yaml
# ❌ YAML sees this as a key-value pair due to the colon after 'https'
url: https://example.com:8080/path  # This will cause an error

# ✅ Use quotes
url: "https://example.com:8080/path"
```

Colon (:) followed by a space is a common problem. As a rule of thumb, if the URL contains:

· a port: :8080
· a fragment: #section
· query parameters like ?x=1&y=2

…wrap it in single or double quotes:

```yaml
login_url: "https://auth.example.com/login?redirect=/dashboard"
doc_url: 'https://docs.example.com#installation'
```

---

Multi-line or very long URLs

Use a block scalar (> or |) to break a long URL without adding line breaks in the actual data.

```yaml
# Folded block (line breaks become spaces)
download_link: >
  https://cdn.example.com/releases/
  v2.3.0/software.zip?token=abc123
```

The value will be:
https://cdn.example.com/releases/ v2.3.0/software.zip?token=abc123 (note the space after /releases/ — be careful).
Better to put the entire URL on one line if possible, or use a literal block only if it genuinely spans multiple lines without breaking the URL.

For a literal block (|), line breaks are preserved (not suitable for a URL unless you really want them).

---

Using YAML anchors for repeated URLs

If the same URL appears many times, you can define it once and reuse it:

```yaml
base: &base_url https://api.example.com/v1
services:
  users: *base_url/users
  orders: *base_url/orders
```

---

Representing a URL as a special tag (optional)

YAML allows custom tags. You could explicitly mark a value as a URL with something like:

```yaml
homepage: !<tag:example.com,2026:url> https://example.com
```

But this is schema-dependent and not part of standard YAML; almost nobody does this.

